<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annapolis Royal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Annapolis Royal is a town on the Annapolis Basin in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. As Port-Royal it was the capital of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, and after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> expedition captured it in 1710 it was renamed after Queen Anne. It served as the capital of British Nova Scotia until Halifax was founded in 1749, and its Fort Anne is now a national historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annapolis-royal.jpg" alt="Annapolis Royal" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dennis G. Jarvis · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lighthouse_DSC02664_-_Annapolis_Royal_Lighthouse_(7986972342).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Annapolis Royal is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and the county seat of Annapolis County, on the Annapolis Basin. Its site, where the Annapolis and Allain rivers meet, is called Nme'juaqnek, 'the place of bountiful fish', by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>, for whom it was for generations a gathering place for chiefs and leaders and a hub on the canoe routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-fundy">Bay of Fundy</a> and the South Shore. 'Port Royal' originally referred to the basin and was named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> in 1604.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The French settlement of Port Royal was founded in 1605 by Pierre Dugua, François Gravé Du Pont and Champlain, at a habitation about ten kilometres west of the present town, and it was the first year-round European settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Attackers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> destroyed it in 1613. In August 1629 Scottish settlers under Sir William Alexander founded Charles Fort on the site of present-day Fort Anne, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1632">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1632. Under French rule it grew modestly, with about 400 people by 1671, and was often attacked and captured, only to be restored by treaty or recapture.</p><p>In 1710, during Queen Anne's War, it was captured for the last time and renamed for the queen. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1713, and from 1710 to 1749 the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> sat there; the Nova Scotia Council was formed there in 1720. French, Acadian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> forces failed to retake it in 1711 and in four attempts during King George's War between 1744 and 1746. In December 1755, 251 men, 263 women and 1,150 children were loaded onto seven ships at Fort Anne and deported. From 1759 to 1768 some 8,000 New Englanders settled in the valley, and Loyalists arrived after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. The fort ceased to be a military post in 1854.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Annapolis Royal was incorporated as a town in 1893. Its core is the Annapolis Royal Historic District National Historic Site, designated in 1994, which includes Fort Anne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s oldest National Historic Site. The town lies within the Southwest Nova Biosphere Reserve, and its economy centres on tourism, heritage and services. The Annapolis Royal Generating Station, a tidal power station, operated from 1984 until 2019, and in 1984 the town elected Daurene Lewis, the first Black woman mayor in Canada.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Annapolis Royal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For decades a small garrison at <b>Annapolis Royal</b> ruled a population of French-speaking Acadians and Mi'kmaq.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a></li></ul></section>
